= Goji Berry (Lycium barbarum)

_Scope: This entry treats the dried ripe fruit of Lycium barbarum L. and L. chinense Mill. (Solanaceae) — the Chinese 枸杞子 (gǒuqǐzǐ), the "goji berry" of modern commerce. Two disambiguations are load-bearing: (1) the fruit 枸杞子 and the root-bark 地骨皮 (dìgǔpí) are two distinct classical Chinese drugs with different actions, both treated here; (2) the botanical genus name Lycium is a false friend — Dioscorides' Greek λύκιον (lykion) was a thorn-shrub eye-medicine (a Rhamnus/Berberis extract), not this plant; Linnaeus merely reused the name. Status: deeply classical in Chinese medicine (and used in Tibetan medicine); reasoned-only in Ayurveda and Tibb (an East-Asian plant outside their canons — though the genus has a native Arabian congener, ʿawsaj), and a post-classical "superfood" in the modern West._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Lycium barbarum_ L. and _Lycium chinense_ Mill. (family Solanaceae); the drug is the *dried ripe fruit* (_Lycii fructus_), and separately the *root-bark* (_Lycii cortex_). The genus name _Lycium_ is taken from Greek *λύκιον (lýkion)* — but *the classical lykion was a different plant*: an astringent extract of a thorny shrub (identified with _Rhamnus_ / _Berberis_ species) from *Lycia* in Asia Minor, used by Dioscorides for the eyes and for wounds. Linnaeus reapplied the ancient name to this Solanaceous genus; the East-Asian goji has *no* classical Greek pedigree. _barbarum_ = "foreign."#footnote[The classical Greek _lýkion_ (Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ I.100) is an astringent extract of a thorny _Rhamnus_/_Berberis_ shrub of Lycia, used for the eyes — *not* the Solanaceous _Lycium_ to which Linnaeus later transferred the name. This is a genuine botanical false-friend: standard history-of-botany references; Beck's Dioscorides commentary. The coincidence that both the classical _lykion_ and modern goji are "eye" drugs is exactly that — a coincidence to be flagged, not a continuity.]

*English:* _Goji_ — a *late-20th/early-21st-century commercial coinage*, an Anglicized spelling of the Mandarin *gǒuqǐ* (枸杞), popularized by the Western "superfood" market from the early 2000s. Older English names: *wolfberry* (a folk rendering, sometimes linked to the _lyk-_/_lykos_ "wolf" false association) and *matrimony vine* (the ornamental shrub).#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "goji, n."): a borrowing of Chinese _gǒuqǐ_, in English use from the early 2000s as a health-food term. "Wolfberry" and "matrimony vine" are the older English names; the "wolf" element is folk-associative, not etymological.]

*Chinese:* *枸杞子 (gǒuqǐzǐ)* — the fruit, the classical drug (子 zǐ = seed/fruit); *枸杞 (gǒuqǐ)* the plant; *地骨皮 (dìgǔpí, "earth-bone bark")* — the *root-bark*, a *separate* classical drug for deficiency-heat. The premier production region gives *寧夏枸杞 (Níngxià gǒuqǐ)* = _L. barbarum_, the Ningxia goji.#footnote[枸杞子 (fruit) and 地骨皮 (root-bark) as two distinct drugs, and 寧夏枸杞 (_L. barbarum_) as the premier commercial source: Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004); Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 枸杞/地骨皮 entries.]

*Sanskrit:* *No classical name* — _Lycium_ is a Chinese/Himalayan-plateau plant outside the dravyaguna canon. Modern Indian usage borrows *गोजी (gojī)* or renders it descriptively; a Himalayan/trans-Himalayan register exists but is not classical Ayurveda. Treated as reasoned-only below.

*Arabic / Persian (Tibb):* *No classical Tibb entry* for the Chinese goji. The genus, however, has a *native Arabian congener*: *عوسج (ʿawsaj)*, the desert boxthorn (_Lycium shawii_ / _L. depressum_), a thorny shrub that appears in Arabic lexicography and in a few hadith as a *thorn-plant* — a different species and not a fruit-tonic drug. Persian for the goji fruit is the modern transliteration or *گوجی (gojī)*. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ`; the ʿawsaj congener is noted below as reasoned extension.#footnote[_ʿawsaj_ (_Lycium shawii_/_depressum_), the Arabian desert boxthorn, is a genus congener attested in Arabic lexicography and in hadith as a thorny shrub — a *different species* from the Chinese goji and not a classical fruit-drug. The Chinese _L. barbarum_ has no medieval Tibb entry; its placement here is reasoned extension.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

Species: _L. barbarum_ / _L. chinense_ (枸杞子, the fruit). Listed in the *superior class (上品)* of the _Shennong Bencao Jing_; a premier *Liver-and-Kidney yin/essence tonic* and *the* classical eye-brightening drug, famed as a longevity food.

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Neutral (平).

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘 gān).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Liver (肝), Kidney (腎), Lung (肺).#footnote[枸杞子 in the _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (神農本草經) superior class; property, flavor, channels: _Bencao Gangmu_ 枸杞 entry; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

*Actions & indications:*
- *滋補肝腎 (zī bǔ gān shèn)* — enriches and tonifies Liver and Kidney yin/essence; for sore aching lower back and knees, dizziness, tinnitus, nocturnal emission, and _xiāokě_ (消渴, wasting-thirst / diabetes-like patterns).
- *益精明目 (yì jīng míng mù)* — augments essence (_jīng_) and *brightens the eyes* — the signature action; for blurred and diminishing vision and dizziness from Liver-Kidney/blood-and-essence deficiency.
- *潤肺 (rùn fèi)* — moistens the Lung; for deficiency/consumptive cough.

*Cautions:* the sweet-moist tonic is contraindicated in Spleen-deficiency with damp and loose stools, and in exterior/damp-heat patterns.

*地骨皮 (the root-bark — a separate drug):* sweet and bland, *cold*; *清虛熱、涼血 (qīng xū rè, liáng xuè)* — clears deficiency (empty) heat and cools the blood; for *骨蒸潮熱 (gǔ zhēng cháo rè,* steaming-bone tidal fever), night sweats, and Lung-heat cough. Keep it firmly distinct from the tonic fruit.#footnote[Fruit actions (滋補肝腎, 益精明目, 潤肺) and the distinct root-bark 地骨皮 (清虛熱涼血, for 骨蒸潮熱): Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The two drugs share a plant but are used oppositely — one a warming-neutral tonic, one a cooling deficiency-heat clearer.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Reasoned analysis, not classical._ Lycium barbarum _is a Chinese/Himalayan plant with no entry in Caraka, Suśruta, or the nighaṇṭus. The reading below applies dravyaguna principles by extension, in the register of the other reasoned-only entries; it slots naturally into the_ cakṣuṣya-rasāyana _class beside_ āmalakī _and_ triphalā.

*Reasoned rasa:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet), with a faint Tikta (तिक्त, bitter) / Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent).

*Reasoned guṇa:* Guru (गुरु, heavy), Snigdha (स्निग्ध, unctuous).

*Reasoned vīrya:* Śīta (शीत, cooling) — the tonic-yin, essence-nourishing register (parallel to drākṣā and āmalakī).

*Reasoned vipāka:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).

*Prabhāva:* None classically (no classical entry). If singled out, the *cakṣuṣya* (eye-benefiting) potency — which modern pharmacology grounds in the fruit's exceptional *zeaxanthin* content — is the candidate feature; flagged as modern interpretive.

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Vāta-Pitta-śāmaka (the sweet-cooling-unctuous profile); increases Kapha in excess.

*Reasoned karma:* _Rasāyana_ (rejuvenative), _cakṣuṣya_ (eye-tonic — the signature, mapping onto the Chinese 明目), _balya_ (strengthening), _vṛṣya_ (aphrodisiac/essence — the 益精 register), _medhya_ (nervine), _brṃhaṇa_ (nourishing). It does not appear in the classical corpus; the placement is reasoned cross-system.#footnote[The reasoned dravyaguna placement anchors on the fruit's Chinese classification (sweet-neutral Liver-Kidney tonic, eye-brightening) and its modern phytochemistry (zeaxanthin, polysaccharides); it is *not* a classical Ayurvedic entry — flagged. The _cakṣuṣya-rasāyana_ analogy is to āmalakī/triphalā, the canonical eye-and-rejuvenation drugs.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(no classical viruddha — extensions of conditional rules; tags S/N/F/M)_

- *(extended) Conditional:* Kapha excess / _āma_ and loose-stool states (the sweet-heavy-moist tonic), paralleling the Chinese Spleen-damp caution.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Warfarin interaction* — *the best-documented modern caution*: goji (juice or fruit) has raised INR and caused bleeding in case reports, probably via CYP2C9 inhibition; caution with warfarin and other anticoagulants.
  - *Pregnancy* — traditional caution (betaine content; the emmenagogue folk register); moderate dietary use is generally considered acceptable, therapeutic doses less so.
  - *Solanaceae / atropine-alkaloid* — the nightshade-family trace-alkaloid concern is minimal for the fruit at food doses but worth noting.
  - *Allergy and pesticide residue* — pollen/fruit allergy occurs; cheap imported goji has carried pesticide-residue concerns.#footnote[The goji–warfarin interaction (raised INR, bleeding; likely CYP2C9-mediated) is documented in clinical case reports and interaction reviews — the best-attested modern caution; verify current literature. Solanaceae-alkaloid content is minimal in the ripe fruit at food doses. Pregnancy caution is traditional/precautionary.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Not classical in Tibb for the Chinese goji_ — _L. barbarum_ was unknown to the medieval Arabic-Persian world. The genus's native Arabian congener *عوسج (ʿawsaj)* (_L. shawii_) is a *different species*, a thorny desert shrub of lexical and minor-hadith note, not a fruit-tonic drug. The placement below is *entirely reasoned* extension on the humoral axis; see `tibb-al-arabi.typ`.

*Reasoned mizāj:* temperate, tending *Hot-Moist in the 1st degree* — a sweet nutritive tonic (by analogy to raisin/_zabīb_ and to the tonic-fruit register).

*Reasoned afʿāl:* *mughadhdhī* (nutritive), *muqawwī al-badan* (body tonic), *muqawwī al-baṣar* (eyesight-strengthener — the reasoned correlate of 明目), *muqawwī al-kabid* (liver tonic), *muqawwī al-bāh* (the essence/aphrodisiac register).

*Reasoned istemālāt:* general debility and convalescence; weak eyesight; the yin/essence-deficiency cluster in humoral translation.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* *none for the Chinese goji.* The Arabian *ʿawsaj* appears in a few narrations as a thorn-shrub, but that is a different species and not a tonic-fruit usage — flagged so as not to lend the modern goji a Prophetic pedigree it does not have. No classical Tibb formula is asserted for _L. barbarum_.#footnote[No Tibb entry exists for _L. barbarum_; the reasoned placement is interpretive. The Arabian congener _ʿawsaj_ (_L. shawii_) has some lexical/hadith presence as a thorn-plant but is a distinct species with no fruit-tonic tradition — the modern goji should not be given a Nabawī pedigree via the congener.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *杞菊地黃丸 (Qǐ Jú Dì Huáng Wán, "Lycium, Chrysanthemum & Rehmannia Pill")* — the six-ingredient Rehmannia pill (六味地黃丸) augmented with goji and chrysanthemum; *the* classical formula for Liver-Kidney yin deficiency with *eye* symptoms (blurred/diminished vision, dry eyes, dizziness). See `rehmannia.typ` for the base formula's chief herb.
- *左歸丸 / 左歸飲 (Zuǒguī Wán / Yǐn)* — goji as an essence-and-yin-tonifying member for Kidney-yin/essence depletion.
- *Food-therapy and tonic wine* — goji in tonic congee and soups, and *枸杞酒 (gǒuqǐ jiǔ,* goji tonic wine); the everyday longevity-food register (the legendary longevity of Li Qingyun is popularly attached to goji — folklore, flagged).
- *地骨皮* formulas (root-bark, separate drug): *清骨散 (Qīng Gǔ Sǎn)* and *地骨皮飲 (Dìgǔpí Yǐn)* for steaming-bone deficiency fever.#footnote[杞菊地黃丸, 左歸丸, and the 地骨皮 deficiency-heat formulas: Bensky & Barolet, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. (Eastland Press, 2009). The Li Qingyun longevity legend is folklore — flagged.]

=== Ayurveda

No classical Ayurvedic formula uses goji. Modern Ayurvedic-adjacent and Himalayan use slots it into the *cakṣuṣya-rasāyana* register (beside āmalakī/triphalā) — reasoned, post-classical.

=== Western / Greco-Roman

*No classical Western goji tradition.* The Greek _lykion_ of Dioscorides is a *different plant* (see Nomenclature) — the name-coincidence must not be read as continuity. The Western goji story is a *21st-century "superfood" phenomenon*: goji entered the Western market as a marketed antioxidant/longevity berry around 2000, with health claims that ran well ahead of the (mostly preclinical and small-trial) evidence — flagged.#footnote[The Greek _lykion_ ≠ goji false-friend (Dioscorides I.100). The Western goji market is a post-2000 "superfood" phenomenon whose marketing outran the evidence; the substantive tradition is Chinese, not Western.]

=== Ṭibb (Arabic-Islamic medicine)

No classical Tibb tradition for _L. barbarum_; the reasoned humoral placement and the ʿawsaj-congener note are covered above.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

_L. barbarum_ is a *cold-arid continental-temperate* plant of northern and northwestern China — *Ningxia, Gansu, Qinghai, Inner Mongolia, Xinjiang* — and the Tibetan-plateau margins, grown in irrigated arid river-valley soils under intense high-altitude sunlight. It is not a tropical, Mediterranean, or humid-temperate crop. See `climates.typ`.

=== Cold-dry continental / high-plateau (the home)

Constitutionally a *Vāta-provoking cold-dry* zone (external 寒/燥; the plateau's dryness and cold), with *intense solar radiation*. *Traditional preparation and cares.* The sweet-moist *yin-and-essence-nourishing dried berry* is exactly the constitutional corrective the frameworks predict for a cold, dry, bright environment — a snigdha, brṃhaṇa, fluid-and-essence-restoring food against the desiccating Vāta climate. There is a satisfying ecological logic to its signature *eye* use: a berry from an extreme-UV, high-glare environment is exceptionally rich in *zeaxanthin*, the very carotenoid the retina uses for photoprotection (see pharmacology) — the plant concentrates, and the eater borrows, a light-stress defense.

=== Tibetan / Himalayan and adoptive global

Goji is used in *Tibetan medicine* and across the Himalayan plateau, and is now shipped globally as a dried "superfood." In non-native climates it is a post-classical import; the *indication* (yin/essence and eye tonic) is climate-neutral, but the classical dose-and-form (measured dried fruit, decoction, or tonic wine) is finer than the modern handful-of-snack register.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26)

Goji's globalization is mostly a *form-and-claim* transposition rather than a climatic mismatch: the traditional *measured tonic* (6–15 g dried fruit, decocted or in wine) becomes a *marketed antioxidant snack* with inflated claims. The genuine cautions travel with it — above all the *warfarin interaction* — and are easily lost in the superfood framing. See `climates.typ`.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Goji is a *dried fruit / tonic-food*; its interest is more pharmacological (carotenoids, polysaccharides) than macronutritional.

*Key constituents:*
- *Zeaxanthin (largely as zeaxanthin dipalmitate) — the headline constituent*: goji is one of the *richest known dietary sources of zeaxanthin*, the macular xanthophyll carotenoid; this is the clean molecular correlate of the classical 明目 / _cakṣuṣya_ (eye-brightening) use — a genuine cross-system convergence.
- *Lycium barbarum polysaccharides (LBP)* — the extensively-studied bioactive polysaccharide-protein fraction (antioxidant, immunomodulatory, neuroprotective in preclinical work).
- Other *carotenoids* (β-carotene), *betaine*, flavonoids, *vitamin C*, and trace minerals.#footnote[Zeaxanthin (as the dipalmitate) as a defining, exceptionally high constituent, plus the LBP fraction, betaine, and carotenoids: current phytochemical and nutrition literature on _Lycium barbarum_ — verify current figures. Goji is among the highest dietary zeaxanthin sources.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Macular / retinal (the eye use)* — zeaxanthin is selectively concentrated in the macula for blue-light photoprotection and antioxidant defense; small human trials of goji have shown increases in macular pigment and plasma zeaxanthin, with plausible relevance to age-related macular degeneration. *This is the honest headline*: the 明目 tradition and the zeaxanthin pharmacology converge cleanly, though clinical endpoints remain modest.
- *Antioxidant / immunomodulatory / neuroprotective* — largely *LBP-driven and largely preclinical*; human evidence is limited and the "superfood" claims are overstated.
- *Metabolic* — modest preclinical/limited-clinical glucose- and lipid-modulating signals.

*Safe intake / dosage:*
- *Traditional Chinese range:* ~*6–15 g dried fruit/day* (decoction, tea, tonic wine, or eaten); food amounts are well tolerated.
- *Cautions / risk groups:* *warfarin / anticoagulants* — the best-documented interaction (raised INR, bleeding); *pregnancy* (traditional caution); allergy; and pesticide-residue concern with low-quality imports.
- *Reference information, not personalized medical advice* — anyone on warfarin should treat goji (fruit or juice) as an interacting food and consult a clinician.#footnote[Traditional dose (~6–15 g/day): Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The warfarin interaction is the best-documented modern caution (case reports; likely CYP2C9). Macular-pigment/zeaxanthin human data are promising but modest; broader antioxidant/immune claims are largely preclinical — flagged. Reference information, not medical advice; verify current literature.]

